[Music]  2020 is shaping up to be a record year  for betting on stock options  on an average day traders drew up more  than 26 million  such contracts a huge rise from the year  before  nine out of ten of the busiest days in  the options market's  entire history going back to 1973  have happened this year in 2020  option contracts give traders a way to  make a buck by  betting on which way the markets will  swing which makes them especially  attractive  when markets are choppy like they've  been this year but there's a catch  options are a risky investment sometimes  even riskier than stocks  and as more beginners try their hand at  trading experts warn of big losses  [Music]  at its most basic level an option is a  contractual agreement between two  parties regarding a particular asset  say a stock and there are two basic  types  calls and puts with a call option  the buyer gets the right to purchase  shares at an agreed upon price  and date if the trader doesn't buy the  shares in that window  she loses the right to buy the shares at  that price  and that's an important feature of these  options contracts you don't have to  exercise them you simply have the option  to do so  within a specific time frame when a  trader buys calls  they're typically making a bet that the  asset will rise in value  if it does she can profit  let's walk through an example shares of  a company are trading at 100  on the day the option contract is bought  a trader secures the option to purchase  100 shares  at 120 dollars each at some point for  the next  month in this scenario 120  is the strike price the trader that buys  the contract  thinks the stock will rise so she pays a  premium  of three dollars per share or for this  contract  300 total many investors are using these  options  contracts as trading tools and often  they don't even have an intention  to exercise them for example if you buy  an auction  and the contract itself rises in value  you might sell that option contract in  the secondary market  in this case the call holder is using  the option contract as a trading tool  not dealing with the underlying shares  but this type of trading can get  complicated  sometimes it's not as simple and that's  because there's a lot of different  inputs  to options pricing that includes the  strike price  that that you're buying the option for  the time to expiration  and also the implied volatility or how  much  the stock is expected to swing up or  down in the future  the option buyer has other ways to  profit as well if the share value rises  higher  than the strike price and the premium  paid the buyer can  exercise the contract in this example  say the share price rises to 135 dollars  which is above the strike price and  premium  combined the call holder can buy the  stocks for 120 dollars  and sell it on the market for 135  dollars  pocketing the difference but let's say  the shares price never rises above 123  dollars  in this case the contract holder doesn't  have to buy the stock  she can toss the contract in the trash  and walk away having only lost the  premium of three dollars per share  but this is where it gets risky for  amateurs these losses can add up quickly  options can seem like a relatively  cheaper way  to sometimes bet on expensive stocks but  you know if if your hunch is wrong a few  times  that can be several hundred dollars at a  time just kind of going down the drain  and potentially burning a hole in your  portfolio you know you may think oh i  just need to put down  200 or 300 for this options premium  and if the stock does this i'll be right  and i can make a quick 40  return but if you're wrong a few times  that's a lot of money for a lot of  people and this happens frequently  many options go unexercised now the  other kind of options trade  is a put in a put contract a trader who  owns a stock  is buying the right to sell that asset  to someone else for a particular price  by a particular date a trader might buy  a put  if they think the stock will fall or if  they want to hedge  their holdings similar to a call option  the trader has the choice to sell  or walk away from the deal and lose the  premium  people often use put options to  bet on stock declines or hedge their  portfolios kind of like a stock  insurance so  a portfolio manager could use a put  option to  you know protect a portfolio from bigger  declines in the s p 500  or something like that so those are the  fundamentals  now here's why options are popular  investors  like options partly because the premium  can let an investor  control more assets than they otherwise  could afford they can be kind of a  cheaper way  to bet on you know somewhat expensive  stocks  for example amazon you know has been  recently trading  around thirty one hundred dollars a  share  meanwhile you could buy an option  contract typed amazon  which technically gives you control over  a hundred shares of amazon  for a few hundred dollars another reason  why options are popular this year  big swings give investors more chances  to bet  the pandemic and election have led to  huge fluctuations in stock prices  which has given traders more  opportunities to bet using option  contracts  another reason people at home have more  time on their hands  and are trading frequently recently  small trades of a single contract  made up 8.7 percent of all options  activity  according to trade alert data those  traders are taking a risk  because making the right call or put is  difficult if not impossible  even for the most advanced traders not  to mention the fees and capital gains  taxes  which can eat into your profits it can  be really tempting to make these short  term relatively cheap bets on where  individual stocks  or the market will go but it's really  hard to figure out what the stock market  is going to do next and  if you guess a few times wrong you could  be out a lot of money  for a smaller investor that means that  consistent success  trading options is tricky regardless  traders are free to swim in the choppy  waters  if they wish  [Music]  you

